      "text": "To find the inverse function \\( f^{-1} \\) of the function \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), we need to determine the composition of the inverse functions of the individual functions \\( p \\), \\( q \\), and \\( r \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\]\n\nWe know that for a function \\( f \\) defined as \\( f(x) = q(p(r(x))) \\), the inverse function \\( f^{-1} \\) can be found by reversing the composition. Specifically, if \\( f(x) = q(p(r(x))) \\), then:\n\\[ f^{-1}(x) = y \\implies x = q(p(r(y))) \\]\nThis means we need to apply the inverse functions in the reverse order of composition. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ f^{-1} = p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\]\n\nTo verify, let's go through the reasoning step-by-step:\n1. If \\( y = f(x) = q(p(r(x))) \\), then to find \\( x \\) such that \\( f^{-1}(x) = y \\), we need to reverse the composition.\n2. First, apply \\( r^{-1} \\) to \\( y \\):\n\\[ r^{-1}(y) = x \\]\n3. Next, apply \\( p^{-1} \\) to \\( r^{-1}(y) \\):\n\\[ p^{-1}(r^{-1}(y)) = p^{-1}(x) \\]\n4. Finally, apply \\( q^{-1} \\) to \\( p^{-1}(r^{-1}(y)) \\):\n\\[ q^{-1}(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(y))) = q^{-1}(p^{-1}(x)) = x \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ f^{-1}(y) = p^{-1}(q^{-1}(r^{-1}(y))) \\]\n\nThis matches the expression \\( p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\).\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for \\( f^{-1} \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{B}\n\\]",
